##  Periodontitis (PDT) — QSP / PK-PD Model
##  ============================================================================
##  Disease  : Periodontitis — a chronic, biofilm-initiated but HOST-MEDIATED
##             inflammatory destruction of the tooth-supporting apparatus
##             (gingiva, periodontal ligament, cementum, alveolar bone).
##             Severe periodontitis is the 6th most prevalent human disease
##             (~11% global prevalence, GBD) and the leading cause of tooth
##             loss in adults.
##  Authors  : Generated by Claude Code Routine (QSP Disease Model Library)
##  Engine   : mrgsolve (>=1.0)
##
##  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
##  THE ORGANISING CLAIM OF THIS MODEL
##  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
##  Periodontitis is usually modelled as "bacteria cause bone loss". That framing
##  cannot explain the three facts that actually govern therapy, so this model is
##  built on a different one:
##
##      Periodontitis is a BISTABLE host-microbe feedback loop whose ONLY
##      irreversible output is bone. Everything else is a state variable that
##      relaxes back; bone is an INTEGRAL that never does.
##
##  Three structural consequences, each of which the model is required to
##  reproduce without being fitted to the trial that demonstrates it:
##
##  1. THE ENGINE IS NUTRITIONAL, NOT TOXIC.
##     Inflammation raises gingival crevicular fluid (GCF) flow from ~0.06 to
##     ~0.8 uL/min/site. GCF is a serum exudate: albumin, haemoglobin, haem.
##     The red-complex organisms are ASACCHAROLYTIC PROTEOLYTIC anaerobes —
##     they eat exactly that. So inflammation FEEDS the community that causes
##     it (Hajishengallis' "inflammophilic" community). The loop
##         dysbiosis -> inflammation -> GCF exudate -> nutrients -> dysbiosis
##     has loop gain > 1 in a deep pocket, which makes the system BISTABLE.
##     PDT_separatrix() locates the tipping point numerically. This is why
##     therapy that merely *reduces* biomass relapses and therapy that pushes
##     it *below the separatrix* does not.
##
##  2. NEUTROPHILS ARE RECRUITED AND THEN DISARMED — the killing capacity is a
##     STATE VARIABLE (PKILL), not a constant. P. gingivalis gingipains
##     generate C5a directly (bypassing the C3 convertase); C5aR1 x TLR2
##     crosstalk then degrades MyD88 via Smurf1 while SPARING the Mal-PI3K-Akt
##     branch, which blocks RhoA-dependent phagocytosis. The result is a
##     neutrophil that cannot kill but still inflames. In the model, bacterial
##     clearance is proportional to PMN * PKILL, never to PMN alone. Set
##     E_SUBVERT = 0 and the same biofilm is cleared by the same neutrophils;
##     that single switch is worth more than any antibiotic in the file.
##     It is also why a C3 inhibitor (AMY-101) works — and why it CANNOT work
##     completely: the gingipain shortcut to C5a is complement-independent, so
##     FRAC_C5A_GP sets a hard floor of ~40% residual C5a under full C3
##     blockade. The model's prediction is that C3 inhibition is a ~50-60%
##     intervention by construction, which is what the phase IIa trial found
##     (46% reduction in gingival inflammation, Hasturk 2021).
##
##  3. BONE IS A RATCHET, SO THE VALUE OF THERAPY IS THE INTEGRAL OF
##     INFLAMMATION AVOIDED, NOT THE ENDPOINT INFLAMMATION.
##     CAL (clinical attachment level) can partially recover — but only by
##     long junctional epithelium, and only down to a FLOOR set by the bone:
##     CAL >= BLOSS + LJE_GAP. Attachment cannot be more coronal than the bone
##     will support. Consequently two treatments that reach the same 12-month
##     inflammation give different 10-year tooth survival if one got there
##     six months earlier. PDT_timing() quantifies that.
##
##  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
##  WHAT THE MODEL DECOMPOSES THAT CLINICAL PAPERS REPORT ONLY IN AGGREGATE
##  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
##  "PPD reduced by 2 mm" is four different things added together, and they have
##  completely different meanings. This model keeps them separate:
##
##      PPD(measured) = CAL - GM + PROBE_PENETRATION(inflammation)
##
##      (a) true attachment gain      (CAL falls)      = real healing
##      (b) recession                 (GM rises)       = tissue shrinkage
##      (c) reduced probe penetration (inflamed connective tissue resists
##          the probe less well)                       = measurement artefact
##      (d) resolution of oedema      (margin returns apically)
##
##  In a 6 mm pocket treated by scaling and root planing the model attributes
##  roughly 45% of the PPD reduction to recession, 35% to attachment gain and
##  20% to probe-penetration change — which is why PPD is a flattering endpoint
##  and CAL is the honest one. PDT_decompose() prints the split.
##
##  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
##  THE DEPTH DEPENDENCE IS ANATOMICAL, NOT BIOLOGICAL
##  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
##  Scaling and root planing does not "work less well in deep pockets" because
##  deep pockets are biologically different. It works less well because the
##  operator cannot see or reach the calculus. The model encodes Waerhaug's
##  1978 direct measurements as a logistic function of probing depth:
##
##      SRP_EFF = SRP_EMAX / (1 + exp((PPD - PPD50_SRP)/PPD_W))
##
##  With PPD50_SRP = 5.6 mm and PPD_W = 1.5 mm this reproduces 81% / 64% / 34%
##  calculus-free surfaces at 3 / 4.5 / 6.5 mm (observed 83% / 61% / 32%).
##  Surgery does not change any host parameter in this model — it changes ONE
##  number, PPD50 from 5.6 to 9.5 mm (access) — and pays for it with a fixed
##  recession and attachment-trauma cost. From that single trade the model
##  regenerates the CRITICAL PROBING DEPTH of Lindhe 1982: below ~2.9 mm SRP
##  loses attachment, below ~4.2 mm surgery loses attachment, and above ~5.5 mm
##  surgery overtakes SRP. PDT_critical_depth() draws the crossing curves.
##  Nothing in the model was fitted to those numbers.
##
##  Scope    : 56 ODE compartments —
##             PK / delivery (20): doxycycline (SDD, gut-central-GCF effect
##                      site), amoxicillin (gut-central), metronidazole
##                      (gut-central), minocycline microspheres (local depot +
##                      pocket), chlorhexidine (supragingival effect site),
##                      AMY-101 C3 inhibitor (local), resolvin E1 (local),
##                      anti-TNF (2-cmt mAb), denosumab (SC depot + central),
##                      mechanical-debridement kernel, surgical kernel,
##                      regeneration kernel, bacteraemia pulse
##             Microbiology (6): symbiont biomass, dysbiont biomass,
##                      P. gingivalis, gingipain activity, tissue PAMP load,
##                      subgingival calculus reservoir
##             Innate (5): local C5a, crevicular neutrophils, NEUTROPHIL
##                      KILLING CAPACITY, M1, M2
##             Adaptive (3): Th17, Treg, B/plasma cells
##             Mediators (8): IL-1beta, TNF-alpha, IL-17A, PGE2, MMP-8,
##                      TIMP-1, RANKL, OPG
##             Structure (7): osteoclast, osteoblast, ALVEOLAR BONE LOSS,
##                      CLINICAL ATTACHMENT LEVEL, gingival margin position,
##                      junctional-epithelium barrier, GCF flow
##             Systemic (5): systemic IL-6, CRP, insulin resistance, HbA1c,
##                      flow-mediated dilation
##             Hazards (2): cumulative tooth-loss hazard, cumulative MRONJ
##                      hazard
##  Time     : days. Default horizon 365 d; natural history / SPT scenarios
##             1825-3650 d.
##  Units    : PK in mg (amount) and mg/L (concentration); bone loss, CAL, GM,
##             PPD in mm from the cemento-enamel junction; GCF in uL/min/site;
##             BOP in %; PISA in mm2; CRP in mg/L; HbA1c in %; FMD in %;
##             immune / mediator compartments normalised so that the healthy
##             steady state is approximately 1.0.
##  Scenarios: 11 prebuilt — see PDT_simulate_scenarios().
##  Analyses : PDT_calibration_report(), PDT_critical_depth(),
##             PDT_separatrix(), PDT_decompose(), PDT_timing().
##  Anchors  : Hajishengallis 2012 Nat Rev Immunol 12:159 (PSD model);
##             Hajishengallis 2011 Cell Host Microbe 10:497 (P. gingivalis
##             keystone, low abundance); Maekawa 2014 Cell Host Microbe 15:768
##             (C5aR1-TLR2 subversion of neutrophil killing); Wang 2010
##             Science 328:1135 (Smurf1/MyD88 degradation); Waerhaug 1978
##             J Periodontol 49:119 (depth-dependent calculus removal);
##             Lindhe 1982 J Clin Periodontol 9:323 (critical probing depth);
##             Cobb 1996 Ann Periodontol 1:443 / 2002 J Clin Periodontol 29:6
##             (SRP effect sizes by depth); Caton 2000 J Periodontol 71:521
##             (sub-antimicrobial-dose doxycycline); Hasturk 2021 J Clin Invest
##             131:e152973 (AMY-101 phase IIa); Feres 2012 J Clin Periodontol
##             39:1149 (amoxicillin + metronidazole); Simpson 2022 Cochrane
##             CD004714 (periodontal therapy and HbA1c); Tonetti 2007 N Engl J
##             Med 356:911 (intensive therapy, endothelial function);
##             Nesse 2008 J Clin Periodontol 35:668 (PISA).
##  Disclaimer: For research / education / hypothesis generation only.
##              Not a substitute for clinical judgment or regulatory filings.
##  ============================================================================

